344-58.value:
The primary manipulated variables are:
- retention access R: off/on;
- history requirement: Markov/current-state sufficient versus history-dependent;
- retained-state intervention: intact, erased, permuted, half-scrambled or opposite-state substitution;
- feedback access F: open-loop versus genuine closed-loop, with yoked and robust-open controls;
- disturbance predictability: predictable versus unpredictable after control onset;
- feedback timing/integrity: delays 1/2/4, masking 25%/50%, noise levels 0.15/0.30, stale observations 2/4, sign scrambling;
- learning access L: off/on;
- behavioural mapping: stable versus changed;
- learning update pathway: intact, disabled/frozen or randomised;
- full factorial R x F x L architecture.
Confirmatory task structure is also frozen: majority_three, mixed_heldout, rotate2_heldout, and cycle_alt_heldout.

344-66.value:
The main derived quantities are paired mechanism effects and specificity interactions:
- retention interaction = [R1-R0 in history task] - [R1-R0 in Markov task];
- feedback interaction = [F1-F0 under unpredictable disturbance] - [F1-F0 under predictable disturbance];
- learning interaction = [L1-L0 after mapping change] - [L1-L0 under stable mapping].
R(tau) is binary mutual information between retained state and the nominated historical distinction at lag tau. Rcausal is the absolute difference in preregistered later performance under two controlled retained-state interventions. No total adaptation, organisation or intelligence index will be constructed.

